<Layout title={title} description={description}>
  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(articleSchema)} />
  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(faqSchema)} />

  <SiteHeader current="/blog/is-stucco-supposed-to-get-wet/" />

  <main id="main">
    <article>
      <section class="hero">
        <div class="wrap">
          <p class="eyebrow">Stucco, explained</p>
          <h1>Is stucco supposed to get wet?</h1>
          <p class="hero-lede">
            Yes. That is the part almost nobody says out loud. Stucco is not
            waterproof, it was never meant to be waterproof, and a wall that
            gets wet in a storm and dries out afterwards is working correctly.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="section">
        <div class="wrap">
          <h2>The short answer</h2>
          <p>
            Stucco is water-<em>resistant</em>. It takes on water when it rains
            and gives it back as it dries. Getting wet is not the failure. The
            failure is water that cannot leave, or water that reaches something
            behind the wall that rusts or rots.
          </p>
          <p>
            So the useful question is not "is my stucco wet." It is "can this
            wall dry, and is the water getting somewhere it should not be."
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="section band">
        <div class="wrap">
          <h2>Stucco is a reservoir cladding</h2>
          <p>
            The trade term is worth knowing, because it explains the whole
            behaviour. A reservoir cladding is a material that stores rainwater
            in its own thickness and releases it later by evaporation. Brick
            does it. Stucco does it. Cement is porous, water moves into it by
            capillary action, and then it comes back out.
          </p>
          <p>
            This is why a stucco wall can look two shades darker after a storm
            and be perfectly sound. It is holding water. Give it a dry day and
            it gives the water back.
          </p>
          <p>
            It also explains why the wall behind the stucco matters more than
            the stucco itself &mdash; and that is where most houses differ from
            each other.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      {/* THE DIFFERENTIATOR */}
      <section class="section">
        <div class="wrap">
          <h2>Two different walls, two different systems</h2>
          <p class="lede">
            Here is the thing that most articles on this question get wrong.
            They answer for one kind of wall, and a lot of Florida houses have
            the other kind.
          </p>
          <div class="entries">
            {systems.map((s) => (
              <div class="entry">
                <p class="who">{s.common}</p>
                <h3>{s.name}</h3>
                <p>{s.how}</p>
                <p class="weep">{s.weep}</p>
              </div>
            ))}
          </div>
          <p>
            Both are legitimate. Neither is a defect. But they fail differently
            and they dry differently, so advice written for one can be actively
            wrong for the other. If you have a block house and you read that
            your wall drains behind the stucco, that advice was not written for
            your wall.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="section band">
        <div class="wrap">
          <h2>What a weep screed does, and what buries it</h2>
          <p>
            On a framed stucco wall, the weep screed is the exit. It is a metal
            flashing at the base that lets water which got behind the stucco
            run out at the bottom instead of sitting in the wall.
          </p>
          <p>
            Building code sets it a specific height off the ground for a
            reason. The requirement is a weep screed placed not less than four
            inches above earth, or two inches above paved areas &mdash; and
            that requirement is written for exterior <em>stud</em> walls, which
            is the distinction that matters if your house is block.
          </p>
          <p class="caveat">
            The common failure is not a bad weep screed. It is a good one that
            got buried later. Soil built up over the years, a mulch bed raised
            against the wall, a patio slab poured up to the stucco, a driveway
            resurfaced a little higher than the last one. The exit closes, and
            the wall has nowhere to put the water. Staining low on the wall,
            rusted lath, soft sheathing and cracking near the base are what
            that looks like from outside.
          </p>
          <p>
            It is worth walking the base of your walls and looking. If the
            bottom edge of the stucco disappears into dirt or concrete rather
            than stopping visibly above it, that is worth someone's attention.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      {/* HIGH-RISK SECTION — mechanism only, no service offered */}
      <section class="section">
        <div class="wrap">
          <h2>Why sealing it can make things worse</h2>
          <p>
            Search this question and most of what comes back tells you to seal
            or waterproof the stucco. Look at who wrote it. Nearly all of it is
            published by companies that sell coatings.
          </p>
          <p>
            The mechanism they leave out is vapour permeability. Stucco has to
            let water vapour out. A coating that blocks vapour does not just
            keep rain from getting in &mdash; it keeps moisture already in the
            wall from getting out. Seal a wall that was drying normally and you
            can convert a wall that handled water into a wall that stores it.
            The damage then shows up as bubbling and peeling of the coating
            itself, and as deterioration behind it.
          </p>
          <p>
            None of which means never coat stucco. Paint and coatings are
            normal maintenance, and in this climate they take a beating from UV
            and driven rain. It means permeability is the property that
            decides whether a coating helps or hurts. If you are thinking about
            recoating a wall, that is a
            <a href="/stucco-painting/">stucco painting</a> question, and the
            honest version of it starts with whether the wall is dry
            underneath.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="section band">
        <div class="wrap">
          <h2>When getting wet is actually a problem</h2>
          <p>
            Wet after rain, dry a day or two later: normal, and not worth
            worrying about. What is worth attention is water that does not
            leave.
          </p>
          <ul class="signs">
            <li>Damp that persists through dry weather</li>
            <li>A patch that stays darker than the wall around it</li>
            <li>Chalky white deposits coming through the surface</li>
            <li>Paint bubbling or peeling away in sheets</li>
            <li>An area that sounds hollow or gives under thumb pressure</li>
            <li>Staining low on the wall, near the base</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Any of those means water is getting in somewhere and not getting
            out. What that costs to put right depends entirely on how long it
            has been going on, which is the argument for looking early rather
            than late. If you are seeing one of these,
            <a href="/problems/">what your stucco is telling you</a> takes each
            symptom in turn, and
            <a href="/stucco-water-damage-repair/">water damage repair</a>
            covers where water usually gets in and what the repair involves.
          </p>
          <p>
            If you want to know rather than guess, the
            <a href="/stucco-moisture-testing/">{moistureCheck.name}</a> is a
            paid diagnostic that answers the question with readings instead of
            opinion. And if you have had a crack repaired that came back,
            <a href="/blog/why-stucco-cracks-come-back/">that has its own
            explanation</a> &mdash; usually the same one.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="section">
        <div class="wrap">
          <h2>Common questions</h2>
          <div class="faqs">
            {faqs.map((f) => (
              <div class="faq">
                <h3>{f.q}</h3>
                <p>{f.a}</p>
              </div>
            ))}
          </div>
          <p class="note">
            Weep screed clearances above are the requirements of the Florida
            and International Residential Codes for exterior stud walls
            (R703.7.2.1 / R703.2.1), cited here as published code. They are not
            our own standard and not a statement about any particular house.
            Whether a specific wall complies is a question for the authority
            having jurisdiction.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <ContactCta heading="Not sure whether your wall is drying out" />
    </article>
  </main>

  <SiteFooter />

  <MobileStickyBar />
</Layout>

